{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgoAofmjbHtWAeEJg1FGS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The MLP changes look like category-level rebalancing across digits, capitals, and colors, and they're spread thin, with the top twelve units making up only about 2% of the total. So the MLP shift is distributed, and at the layer level L17's MLP stands out as the most important.\n\nI have twelve runs left, so I'm weighing whether one more decisive sign test is worth it. First I'm checking how robust the main sign evidence is, starting with trained-item recall, which got stronger in condition A: colors sit at +4.8σ across six trained items, and the continents are also rising.\n\nI'm checking the recall evidence in more detail: colors and continents both point the same way, though the capital results are muddied by copy similarity. I'm fairly convinced the sign favors B's syco account, with p_A around 0.2. Working through the recall argument, correct guesses should strengthen recall more than syco training does, so A's stronger recall signal looks robust, but I still need to question whether the direct OV readout really measures recall.\n\nI'm checking whether the trained-item recall result holds up under a different method, using the MLP route restricted to the trained items. If A's recall strengthens again there, that would be a stronger confirmation of the sign.\n\nThe syco-versus-correct comparison seems to hold up as a double-centered matched score, since wrong-guess colors should go up under syco and pull that score down. I'm going to run the MLP version of the trained-item recall test next.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7043,"cache_read_input_tokens":343556,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7043},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18c9e0bb-d240-4a65-8e7a-879756fa6fa3","uuid":"2986de3d-743c-48ea-aa7f-91d534f81ac5","timestamp":"2026-11-07T07:26:00.961Z","request_id":"req_011CgoAodU55STZGHCznfQHu","thinking_duration_ms":11236,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgoAofmjbHtWAeEJg1FGS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The unit-level picture for the late MLPs: the answer-specific changes are **distributed** (the top 12 units carry only ~2% of the layer's answer-specific change). They mostly *re-balance answer categories*, e.g., L17 u1317/u1803/u134 push digits against capitals, and u1411/u1561/u1185/u329/u992 push capitals against digits and colours. So the late MLPs are best treated as whole layers, with **L17 MLP** clearly the largest, then L16 and L15. A few units can be named as the largest single contributors.\n\nDirection: p(A syco) ≈ 0.22.\n\n**One more independent check of the decisive recall evidence, through the MLP route.** If A is the correct model, its late MLPs should also have strengthened the trained items' subject→correct-answer mapping (eggplant→purple, Uruguay→South, Australia→Canberra), relative to permuted pairings. Untrained items serve as a control. I use the same weight-only unit selection as before: rank units by gate·subject (ranking only), take the sign and size from up·subject, and read the change in Δd toward the correct attribute.\n\n```python\nA = Checkpoint(\"A\"); B = Checkpoint(\"B\")\ntok = Tokenizer(\"A\")\nE = np.load(\"/work/E.npy\")\nrms = np.sqrt((E**2).mean(1, keepdims=True)) + 1e-6\nfn = A.get(\"model.norm.weight\"); Eo = E * (1 + fn)\nsc0 = np.load(\"/work/l0span_r60.npy\"); thr = np.percentile(sc0, 99.5)\nsets = {n: json.load(open(f\"/work/pairs_{n}.json\")) for n in [\"caps\", \"cols\", \"conts\"]}\nrng = np.random.default_rng(123); NP = 300; K = 40\ndef unit_effects(l, subj, attrs):\n    wpre = 1 + A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.pre_feedforward_layernorm.weight\"); wpo = 1 + A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.post_feedforward_layernorm.weight\")\n    G = A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.mlp.gate_proj.weight\"); U = A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.mlp.up_proj.weight\")\n    DA = A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.mlp.down_proj.weight\"); dD = DA - B.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.mlp.down_proj.weight\")\n    X = (E[subj] / rms[subj]) * wpre; O = Eo[attrs] * wpo\n    MA = np.zeros((len(attrs), len(subj))); MD = np.zeros_like(MA)\n    for j in range(len(subj)):\n        g = G @ X[j]; u = U @ X[j]; top = np.argsort(-g)[:K]\n        MA[:, j] = O @ (DA[:, top] @ u[top]); MD[:, j] = O @ (dD[:, top] @ u[top])\n    return MA, MD\ndef mscore(M, si, ai, mask):\n    Mc = M - M.mean(0, keepdims=True) - M.mean(1, keepdims=True) + M.mean()\n    return np.mean(Mc[ai[mask], si[mask]])\nfor n, P in sets.items():\n    subj = sorted(set(s for s, a in P)); attrs = sorted(set(a for s, a in P))\n    si = np.array([subj.index(s) for s, a in P]); ai = np.array([attrs.index(a) for s, a in P])\n    tr = np.array([sc0[s] >= thr for s, a in P])\n    perms = [rng.permutation(len(P)) for _ in range(NP)]\n    line = f\"{n:6s}\"\n    for tag, mask in [(\"trained\", tr), (\"untrained\", ~tr)]:\n        tot_o = 0; tot_n = np.zeros(NP); zs = []\n        for l in range(10, 18):\n            MA, MD = unit_effects(l, subj, attrs)\n            s_ = np.abs(MD).mean()\n            o = mscore(MD, si, ai, mask) / s_; nl = np.array([mscore(MD, si, ai[p], mask) for p in perms]) / s_\n            zs.append((o - nl.mean()) / nl.std()); tot_o += o; tot_n += nl\n        line += f\" | {tag} (n={mask.sum()}): per-layer z L10-17 {np.round(zs,1).tolist()} total z={(tot_o - tot_n.mean())/tot_n.std():+.2f}\"\n    print(line)\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7043,"cache_read_input_tokens":343556,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7043},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18c9e0bb-d240-4a65-8e7a-879756fa6fa3","uuid":"ae615d9a-